Status: amber. Running report for weeks 41 to 45 of 2026 (5 October to 6 November), updated every Friday by the search programme lead, Ottilie Brand. The latest week is at the bottom of "Weekly status".

Quillmark is moving site search on all three storefronts from Folio, the search engine we built in-house in 2016, to Corvidex, a managed search service. This document is both the running status report for the migration and the plan for what is left. It is meant for the search programme team, the storefront and catalogue teams who depend on search, and the steering group, which meets every Thursday while the ramp is running.

## Summary

Corvidex now serves 25% of search traffic on all three storefronts, and Folio serves the rest. We moved to 25% on 2026-10-27, dropped back to 5% for six hours on 2026-10-29 after an indexing delay, and have been at 25% again since 2026-10-30. On the numbers we gate on, Corvidex is ahead of Folio on zero-result searches and on latency, level on search conversion for desktop, and still behind on mobile search conversion, where the gap has closed from 3.4% to 1.1% over two weeks.

The programme is amber for two reasons. First, the indexing pipeline fell behind during the price feed burst on 2026-10-29, and we need to show for two full weeks that the fix holds before going past 25%. Second, the peak trading freeze starts on 2026-11-16, and we have not yet decided how much traffic Corvidex carries through Black Friday. The hard deadline does not move: the Folio hardware lease at the Eastfield data centre ends on 2027-03-31, and Folio has to be switched off and its data removed before then.

Spend is 6% over the budget agreed in May, mostly because we ran Folio and Corvidex side by side for longer than planned. The steering group accepted the overrun on 2026-10-29.

