## Background (history, for people new to the programme) {agent=skip}

Folio was built in 2016 by a team of four, when Quillmark sold only new books on one storefront. It started as a thin layer over an open-source search library, with our own code for ISBN handling, author name matching and a ranking formula that mostly sorted by sales rank. Over the next eight years it grew to handle the marketplace, the Irish and Dutch storefronts, gift cards and audiobooks, and most of the people who knew the ranking code have left. By 2024 Folio ran on 18 servers in the Eastfield data centre, rebuilt its whole index every night in a job that took about 5 hours, and picked up changes during the day through a separate delta job every 20 minutes.

The trigger for this programme was Black Friday 2025. Folio's query servers ran out of memory at 09:40 on the Friday morning, during a promotion on boxed sets, and search was down for 52 minutes across all three storefronts. Shoppers who cannot search mostly leave. Our estimate of the lost sales for that hour was a little over £380,000, and the incident review found that we could not add capacity to Folio quickly, because each new query server needed a full copy of the index built on the server itself, which took most of a day.

In January 2026 the steering group asked for options. We looked at rebuilding Folio on newer hardware, at running an open-source search engine ourselves in the cloud, and at three managed search services. The comparison is in the options paper from February, which is still worth reading if you want to know why the other managed services lost. In short, Corvidex was the only one that handled our ISBN and edition grouping without custom plugins, and it let us keep relevance rules in our own repository rather than in a vendor console. The contract was signed in May 2026 for three years, with a break clause after the first year.

Between May and September the programme built the indexer, moved the storefronts onto the search gateway one at a time, wrote the first ranking rules and assembled the judged query set. The UK storefront moved onto the gateway in July, the Dutch in August and the Irish only in week 41, later than the others because its storefront code was still on an older framework that the web team was replacing anyway. The summer was slower than planned, mostly because the judged query set needed far more grading than anyone expected, and because two of the seven people on the programme were pulled into the checkout rebuild for most of August.

The original plan from May aimed to switch all traffic in one go in October, well ahead of peak trading. That plan changed in week 41, and the reasons are in the weekly notes below.

