## Goals and non-goals

The migration is done when all of these are true.

- All search traffic on the UK, Irish and Dutch storefronts is served by Corvidex.
- The 95th percentile response time of the search gateway is at or under 250 ms, measured at the gateway, for 14 days in a row.
- The zero-result rate, meaning the share of searches that return nothing, is under 4% on every storefront.
- Search conversion, the share of search sessions that end in a purchase, is no worse than Folio's was over the same weeks of the previous year, adjusted for overall traffic.
- A catalogue change, including a price or stock change, shows in search within 5 minutes of the catalogue service publishing it.
- Folio is switched off, and its data is removed from the Eastfield servers before the lease ends.

Search inside the text of books is not part of this migration. Folio never offered it, and the publisher agreements we have do not allow us to index book text for search in any case. Personalised ranking is also out of scope for the first phase. Corvidex can rank results using a shopper's purchase history, but we have not done the privacy review, and the first phase ranks every shopper's results the same way.

Marketplace listings from third-party sellers are not indexed as separate documents in the first phase. A marketplace listing shows up in search only through the edition it belongs to, as "also available from sellers" on the result card. The exception is signed and first editions, which sellers list as their own items and which collectors search for directly, so those listings are indexed as separate documents with their own result cards. There are about 210,000 of them.

Gift cards, stationery and the small range of toys we sell are indexed, but they are kept out of results for searches that look like an ISBN or an author name. Audiobooks are indexed with their print editions and grouped with them under the same work.

## Architecture

Search on all three storefronts goes through the search gateway, a small service that Bram's team owns. The storefronts never call Folio or Corvidex directly. The gateway turns a shopper's query into a request for one of the two engines, applies the business rules that do not belong in either engine (such as hiding titles that a storefront cannot sell), and returns a common result format. Since week 40 the gateway also decides which engine a shopper's searches go to.

The router inside the gateway assigns each shopper to an engine by a hash of their session ID, so a shopper stays on the same engine for the whole session, and the ramp percentage decides how many hash buckets go to Corvidex. There are 1,000 buckets. At 25%, buckets 0 to 249 go to Corvidex. A shopper's assignment does not change when the percentage goes up, except that more buckets join Corvidex, and when we roll back every bucket returns to Folio at once.

Queries sent to Corvidex must not contain the shopper's email address, account ID or postcode. The gateway sends only the query text, the storefront, the shopper's language, the device type and the session hash. Corvidex keeps query logs for 30 days, and its contract allows it to use them only for running our service.

The gateway waits at most 800 ms for an engine to answer. After that it returns the category page for the query's best-matching category instead of a results page, and logs a timeout. At 25% traffic Corvidex timeouts have run at about 0.02% of queries, against 0.09% for Folio.

```mermaid
flowchart LR
  subgraph Catalogue
    CAT[Catalogue service] --> FEED[Change feed]
    PRICE[Price and stock service] --> FEED
  end
  FEED --> IDX[Indexer]
  IDX -- full and partial updates --> CVX[(Corvidex indexes)]
  FEED --> FDELTA[Folio delta job]
  FDELTA --> FOLIO[(Folio)]
  SHOP[Storefronts] --> GW[Search gateway]
  GW --> ROUTER{Router: session hash bucket}
  ROUTER -- buckets below ramp --> CVX
  ROUTER -- other buckets --> FOLIO
```

Each storefront has its own Corvidex index, because the three sites sell different ranges and the Dutch site searches in two languages. Corvidex allows at most 40 million documents per index and at most 8 indexes on our contract tier. The UK index is the largest at 15.1 million documents. Each index has one replica in a second zone, and Corvidex promises 99.95% availability for queries per calendar month.

The indexer is a new service owned by Dev's team. It reads the catalogue change feed, builds index documents, and sends them to Corvidex. Corvidex accepts at most 1,000 documents in one update request and at most 10 update requests per second per index, which caps a single index at 10,000 document updates per second. Folio keeps its own nightly rebuild and delta job until it is switched off, and it reads the same change feed through the old Folio delta job.

## Data and indexing

### What goes into the index

The catalogue holds about 14.2 million editions, grouped into about 3.1 million works, with around 260,000 authors and contributors. An edition is one ISBN, or one product code for items without an ISBN. A work is the book itself, so a hardback, a paperback, an ebook and an audiobook of the same novel are four editions of one work. Search results show works, with the best edition for the shopper picked out and the other formats listed under it. Folio did this grouping at query time, which is one of the reasons it was slow. Corvidex does it with a grouping field at index time, and the indexer writes the work ID onto every edition document.

Every edition becomes one index document on each storefront that sells it. An edition that is not for sale on a storefront is not indexed there, even if the storefront could show it as "currently unavailable". The exception is pre-orders. Titles that are announced but not yet published are indexed from the day the publisher's metadata arrives, so that shoppers can find and pre-order them, and they are marked as pre-orders on the result card.

Titles restricted in a region, for legal or rights reasons, are left out of that region's index entirely. The catalogue service sets the restriction per storefront, and the indexer deletes the document from the affected index within the normal 5 minute freshness target. About 3,400 editions are restricted on at least one storefront, most of them on the Irish storefront because of distribution rights.

A trimmed index document for the UK storefront looks like this. The real documents have about 40 fields.

```json
{
  "id": "uk:9780000000017",
  "work_id": "w-1184403",
  "storefront": "uk",
  "title": "The Lantern Keeper's Daughter",
  "subtitle": null,
  "contributors": [
    {"name": "Maren Holloway", "role": "author"},
    {"name": "Teo Akande", "role": "narrator"}
  ],
  "format": "paperback",
  "series": {"name": "Harbour Lights", "number": 3},
  "language": "en",
  "categories": ["fiction/historical", "fiction/family-saga"],
  "price_pence": 899,
  "in_stock": true,
  "preorder": false,
  "sales_rank_30d": 1874,
  "published": "2025-04-10",
  "marketplace_offers": 4
}
```

Prices are stored in the smallest unit of the storefront's currency, as integers. The Irish and Dutch storefronts use euro cents. The index does not hold any cost price, margin or supplier field. Those were in Folio's index for an old merchandising report, and the finance team asked us not to carry them over.

### Freshness

A change in the catalogue has to show in search within 5 minutes of the catalogue service publishing it to the change feed. That target covers new titles, metadata corrections, price changes, stock changes and restrictions. Folio's delta job ran every 20 minutes, so Folio was often 20 to 30 minutes behind, and shoppers sometimes saw a price in the results that did not match the product page. With the indexer running continuously, the median delay on Corvidex is 41 seconds and the 99th percentile was 3 minutes 10 seconds over week 45.

The catalogue publishes about 2 million changes on an ordinary day, and the price and stock service adds another 5 to 6 million. Most of the price and stock changes come in bursts, when a supplier sends a new price file or the warehouse system posts its stock counts. The largest burst so far was 3.8 million changes in 40 minutes on 2026-10-29, which is the cause of the incident described in the week 44 notes.

Since week 45 the indexer sends price and stock changes as partial updates, which change only those fields in a document, while metadata changes still rewrite the whole document. Partial updates count against the same limit of 1,000 documents per request and 10 requests per second per index, but they are about eight times cheaper for Corvidex to apply, so they do not slow down queries the way full rewrites did.

### Full reindexing

We do a full reindex of a storefront when the index schema changes, when we change how text is analysed (for example a new stemming rule for Dutch), or when we suspect the index has drifted from the catalogue. A full reindex of the UK index takes about 4 hours at the maximum update rate. Corvidex lets us build the new index alongside the old one and swap them in one step, so shoppers never see a half-built index.

:::danger
> **Warning:** A full reindex must not run between 07:00 and 23:00 UK time. It uses the whole Corvidex update quota for the storefront, and price and stock changes queue behind it, so shoppers see wrong prices and can buy items that are out of stock.
:::

A full reindex of all three storefronts needs about 9 hours, so it does not fit in one night. Run one storefront per night, the UK last. During the peak trading freeze no full reindex runs at all, unless the search on-call and the programme lead both agree that the live index is damaged.

### Synonyms and spelling

Each storefront has its own synonym list, kept in the `search-relevance` repository and loaded into Corvidex by the deploy pipeline. A synonym list can have at most 5,000 entries. The UK list is at 3,960 entries today, many of them carried over from Folio, where nobody had removed an entry since 2019. Saoirse's team is going through them, and has removed about 700 so far that either did nothing or made results worse.

Spelling correction is done by Corvidex from the catalogue's own vocabulary, so author names and invented words from fantasy titles are corrected towards what we actually sell. We do not correct queries that look like an ISBN, an order number or a gift card code. Folio corrected ISBNs by one digit, which sent shoppers to the wrong book often enough that customer service had a saved reply for it.

The Dutch storefront searches in Dutch and English, because about 40% of what it sells is English-language stock. The Dutch index analyses every title in both languages and ranks matches in the shopper's site language first. Folio did not do this, and Dutch shoppers searching in English often got nothing, which explains a large part of Folio's high zero-result rate on that storefront.

### Data that stays out of search

Some catalogue data never goes into a search index, on either engine. Customer reviews are not indexed as text, although the average rating and the number of reviews are. Internal notes from the buying team, which some editions carry in the catalogue, are not indexed. Cover images are not indexed, and Corvidex's image features are switched off on our account. Out-of-print editions with no marketplace offers are removed from the index 90 days after the last copy sold, unless they belong to a work that still has an edition for sale, in which case they stay listed under that work as "out of print".

## Relevance and ranking

### How results are ordered

Corvidex scores every matching document on text relevance first, then our ranking rules adjust the score. The rules live in the `search-relevance` repository as configuration, are reviewed like code, and are deployed by the same pipeline as the synonym lists. Nobody changes ranking in the Corvidex console, and the console accounts for our team have read-only rights for ranking settings.

The ranking signals in the first phase are:

- text match on title, then contributors, then series name, then subtitle and categories, with title matches weighted highest;
- sales over the last 30 days on that storefront, with a ceiling so that one bestseller does not push every other match off the first page;
- whether the work has an edition in stock, since shoppers rarely buy a book that ships in three weeks;
- the publication date, for queries that look like they are about new books (for example a query that names an author who has a new title in the last 60 days);
- the format the shopper picked last time on that device, which is stored in the browser and sent by the gateway as a format hint, not taken from the shopper's account.

Folio had 140 hand-written boost rules, most of which pinned particular editions to the top of particular queries. We carried over 22 of them, all for queries where the obvious match is not the book shoppers want (for example a classic novel where the public domain ebook is the best text match but the annotated school edition is what people buy). The rest were either for promotions that ended years ago or did not change anything in Corvidex.

Pinned results, where merchandising puts a title first for a query, are limited to 3 per query and to 200 queries per storefront. Merchandising asked for more during the summer, and the answer was no, because pinned results were a large part of why Folio's results drifted away from what shoppers wanted. A pinned result must have an end date no more than 6 weeks after it starts.

### Measuring relevance

We measure relevance in two ways. The offline way is a judged query set: 2,400 queries taken from real search logs, weighted towards the most common queries, with each of the top 10 results for each query graded by two people from the customer service and buying teams. The online way is the A/B comparison between the two engines on live traffic, using the gate metrics in "Quality gates".

The judged query set gives a score between 0 and 1. Folio scores 0.61 on it. Corvidex with our week 45 rules scores 0.74. A ranking change is not deployed if it lowers the score by more than 0.01, even if it fixes a specific complaint, unless the programme lead agrees it in writing. The full judged set takes about 25 minutes to run, and it runs on every pull request to the relevance repository.

| Query type | Share of searches | Folio zero-result rate | Corvidex zero-result rate (week 45) |
|-|-|-|-|
| Title words | 41% | 3.9% | 2.7% |
| Author name | 23% | 5.2% | 3.1% |
| ISBN | 9% | 1.1% | 0.4% |
| Series name or number | 6% | 14.8% | 9.6% |
| Subject or genre | 12% | 9.3% | 7.2% |
| Other (gift cards, stationery, misspelled) | 9% | 17.4% | 11.9% |
| All queries | 100% | 6.8% | 4.6% |

Series queries are still the weakest. A shopper who types "harbour lights 3" or "harbour lights book three" expects the third book in the series, and both engines are poor at matching the number. Saoirse's team has a fix in testing that reads series numbers out of the query, described in the week 43 and week 45 notes.

### Autocomplete

Autocomplete suggests up to 8 completions on desktop and 4 on mobile, after the shopper has typed at least 2 characters. It waits 120 ms after the last key press before it sends a request, so a fast typist sends one request rather than one per letter. Suggestions come from a separate, smaller Corvidex index that holds only titles, author names and series names, about 4.2 million entries for the UK storefront, and it is updated from the same change feed as the main index.

Suggestions are ordered by text match and then by sales over the last 30 days, since the fix in week 44. Titles that are out of stock on every format are still suggested, because shoppers often want to pre-order or join a waiting list, but they are ordered after titles that are in stock. Suggestions never include gift cards, toys or stationery, and they never include titles that have a content warning flag in the catalogue, even when the shopper has typed the full title. Those titles still appear in the full results after the shopper presses search.

Folio's autocomplete was a separate in-house service, Folio Suggest, which runs on two of the Folio servers. It is switched off together with Folio and needs no plan of its own.

Autocomplete requests are not counted in the query volume that Corvidex bills for, under a clause we negotiated in the contract. They are about 2.5 times as many as full searches, so this matters for the budget.

### Rejected and parked ideas

:::decision{status=rejected date=2026-08-12}
Decision (rejected 2026-08-12): let merchandising manage pinned results and boosts directly in the Corvidex console, so that changes for promotions can go live in minutes rather than through a pull request. Changes made in the console are not reviewed, are not in our repository, and are lost when we rebuild an index from configuration. Merchandising now raises a pull request through a form that generates it, and a change can be live within an hour in working hours.
:::

Corvidex's semantic matching, which finds results by meaning rather than words, is switched off for the first phase. In testing it helped for vague queries like "books about grief for teenagers" and hurt for exact ones, mainly by mixing in titles with similar themes when the shopper had typed a precise title. We will test it again in 2027 for subject queries only.

Ranking by margin was proposed by the commercial team in June. It is not part of this migration. The steering group said that ranking by margin should be decided separately from the engine change, so that any drop in conversion can be blamed on the right change.

## Cutover plan

:::decision{status=superseded date=2026-06-17}
Decision (accepted 2026-06-17): all three storefronts switch from Folio to Corvidex in a single step on Monday 2026-10-19, at 06:00 UK time, after two weeks in which both engines index the full catalogue and Corvidex answers a mirrored copy of live queries without its results being shown. A single switch keeps the measurement simple and means the storefront teams only have to watch one cutover. Folio stays running, without traffic, for six weeks after the switch so that we can go back to it.
:::

The steps for a switch, whichever way the traffic moves, are the same.

1. The programme lead posts in #search-programme which step is planned and when, at least one working day ahead.
2. Ifeoma confirms that the gate report for the previous three days is green for the current percentage.
3. Lukas checks that the Corvidex indexes are within the freshness target and that the Folio delta job is still running, so that rollback to Folio returns shoppers to a fresh index.
4. Bram changes the ramp percentage in the gateway configuration and deploys it. The change reaches all gateway instances within 2 minutes.
5. The on-call watches the gate dashboard for the first hour, and Ifeoma checks the gate report again the next morning.

A ramp step can only happen on a Tuesday or a Wednesday, between 09:00 and 15:00 UK time, so that the whole team is around for the rest of the working week. Rolling back is different. Anyone on the search on-call rota can roll back to Folio at any time, on any day, without asking first, and tells the programme lead afterwards.

Rollback sets the ramp percentage to 0 in the gateway, which sends every bucket to Folio. It has to be possible within 5 minutes at every stage of the ramp. We have tested it three times on production, the last time on 2026-11-03, and it took between 2 and 3 minutes each time, almost all of it the gateway configuration rollout.

```yaml
# gateway/config/router.yaml (production, as of 2026-11-06)
router:
  buckets: 1000
  hash: session_id
  engines:
    corvidex:
      percent: 25
      timeout_ms: 800
      storefronts: [uk, ie, nl]
    folio:
      percent: 75
      timeout_ms: 800
      storefronts: [uk, ie, nl]
  sticky_session: true
  fallback_on_timeout: category_page
  # Never set both engines to serve the same bucket.
  # Rollback: set corvidex.percent to 0 and folio.percent to 100.
```

## Quality gates

Every ramp step needs the gates below to have been green for the current percentage for three days in a row. The gate report compares shoppers on Corvidex with shoppers on Folio over the same days, so seasonal swings affect both sides equally.

| Gate | Measure | Threshold |
|-|-|-|
| Latency | 95th percentile at the gateway | At or under 250 ms |
| Errors | Engine errors and timeouts | Under 0.1% of queries |
| Zero results | Share of searches with no results | At or below Folio's rate for the same days |
| Conversion | Search sessions ending in a purchase | No more than 2% lower than Folio, relative, on each device type |
| Freshness | 99th percentile delay from change feed to index | Under 5 minutes |
| Revenue per search | Revenue from search sessions divided by searches | No more than 2% lower than Folio, relative |

The conversion and revenue gates are relative, so a 2% lower conversion means, for example, 4.9% against 5.0%, not 3.0% against 5.0%. We need at least 40,000 search sessions on each side and each device type before a gate result counts, which at 1% traffic took about four days and at 5% takes less than one.

:::warning
> **Important:** Do not ramp past 25% until the freshness gate has been green for 14 days in a row on all three storefronts, not the usual 3. This applies from the 2026-10-29 incident onwards.
:::

The latency gate has an exception for the Dutch storefront, where the target is 300 ms instead of 250 ms, because every query is analysed in two languages. The exception lasts until the end of the first phase and has to be agreed again after that.

When a gate turns red on a day where the ramp is not changing, nothing happens automatically. The on-call looks at it the same day. If the conversion or revenue gate is red for two days in a row, the programme lead decides whether to roll back.

### Testing before a ramp step

Before each ramp step, and before each relevance release, the change goes through the staging environment. Staging has its own Corvidex indexes for all three storefronts, fed from a copy of the catalogue that is refreshed every Sunday, and a gateway configured the same way as production. Staging indexes are on a smaller Corvidex tier, so latency there is not a guide to production latency, and nobody should quote staging latency in a gate discussion.

The checks on staging before a step are:

- the judged query set runs against the staging indexes and scores at least as well as production;
- the 300 most common queries of the last week return a non-empty first page on every storefront;
- a scripted set of 60 shopper journeys, from search to basket, passes on desktop and mobile web;
- the rollback is run once, from the step's percentage back to 0.

Accessibility is checked on staging for every change to the results page or the autocomplete list. The results page must be usable with a keyboard alone and with a screen reader, and the autocomplete list must announce the number of suggestions. A change that fails the accessibility check does not go to production, whatever its effect on conversion. The accessibility team's sign-off is not needed for a ramp step that only changes the percentage, because the results page is the same for both engines.

Load tests run against production Corvidex, not staging, in the quietest hour of the night, with traffic marked as test traffic so that it is billed at a reduced rate and left out of the gate report. The last load test, on 2026-10-15, ran 2,400 queries per second against the UK index for 30 minutes, which is a little over the 1,900 per second we saw at the worst moment of Black Friday 2025, with a 95th percentile of 188 ms. A load test on production needs the programme lead's approval and must not run during the peak trading freeze.

