The dates after the freeze depend on the open question about peak traffic. If the steering group decides to carry 50% through the peak, the 50% step moves to 2026-11-11 and the plan otherwise stays the same. No ramp step of any size happens during the freeze.

## Storefront notes

The three storefronts share the engine, the gateway and the indexer, but they are not the same, and some of what is in this document applies to one of them only.

The UK storefront carries about 78% of search traffic and almost all of the marketplace listings. It also hosts the business pages for trade and library customers, whose bulk ISBN search sends up to 500 ISBNs in one request. Corvidex handles these as a single filter query rather than 500 searches, so a bulk search counts as one query for billing. Bulk searches are left out of the gate report, because a library uploading a list of 500 ISBNs is not a shopper.

The Irish storefront carries about 9% of search traffic. It has the most restricted titles, about 2,900 of the 3,400, mostly because of distribution rights held by a different company in Ireland. Prices are in euro, and the storefront shares the UK's English synonym list with about 120 Irish additions, mostly place names and Irish language titles, held in a separate overlay file. The overlay counts towards the 5,000 entry limit of the Irish list.

The Dutch storefront carries about 13% of search traffic and searches in two languages, as described in "Synonyms and spelling". It has its own synonym list in Dutch with about 1,700 entries, plus the English list. It also has the most marketplace sellers per edition, because of a large group of second-hand booksellers who joined the marketplace in 2025, so its result cards show "also available from sellers" more often than the other storefronts. Dutch book prices are fixed by law for new books, so the price signals that the UK uses in promotions do not apply there, and the Dutch ranking rules have no promotion boosts at all.

All three storefronts move to Corvidex at the same percentage today. The gateway allows a different percentage per storefront, and we may use that during the peak if the Irish zero-result question is still open, by keeping the Irish storefront at a lower percentage than the others.

## Weekly status

Each week's entry is written on the Friday and not changed afterwards, except to fix a wrong number, which is marked as a correction. Read them in order if you want the full story, or the latest one if you only want where we are.

### Week 41 (5 to 9 October): change of cutover plan

Overall: amber.

The mirrored traffic test ended on Tuesday. For two weeks Corvidex answered a copy of every live query on all three storefronts, without its results being shown to anyone, and we compared its answers and timings with Folio's. Latency was good, with a 95th percentile of 162 ms on the UK storefront. The comparison of results was harder to read than we expected. On 31% of queries the two engines returned a different first result, and the judged query set could only tell us which was better for the queries it contains. For the rest we had no way of knowing which engine shoppers would prefer without showing them.

That result is why the cutover plan changed. A single switch on 2026-10-19 would have moved every shopper at once to an engine that ranks a third of queries differently, with no way of measuring the effect except by comparing against last year's numbers, which are distorted by the 2025 outage and by this year's new Dutch range. Ifeoma and Saoirse proposed a stepped ramp with a live A/B comparison instead, and the steering group agreed it on Friday 2 October, the week before this report.

:::decision{status=accepted date=2026-10-02}
Decision (accepted 2026-10-02): traffic moves from Folio to Corvidex in steps of 1%, 5%, 25%, 50% and 100%, chosen by session hash in the search gateway, with each step gated on the quality gates holding for three days at the previous step. This replaces the single-switch cutover on 2026-10-19 agreed on 2026-06-17. Folio keeps serving the remaining traffic during the ramp, and after the 100% step it stays running without traffic for 30 days, not six weeks, so that it can be switched off before the lease ends.
:::

The full catalogue backfill into Corvidex finished on Wednesday for all three storefronts: 15.1 million documents in the UK index, 9.8 million in the Irish index and 11.3 million in the Dutch index. A check against the catalogue found 1,206 editions missing from the UK index. All of them were editions whose publisher metadata had a title longer than 1,024 characters, which the indexer rejected. Most are academic conference proceedings with the whole table of contents in the title field. The indexer now cuts titles at 1,024 characters for indexing, and the full title is still shown on the product page.

Other work this week:

- Bram's team finished moving the Irish storefront onto the search gateway, so all three storefronts now call search through the gateway. Shoppers saw no change, since the gateway still sent everything to Folio.
- Saoirse found that the Dutch synonym list had been loaded with the UK list's entries appended by mistake, because of a path error in the deploy pipeline. It was fixed the same day, and a check in the pipeline now refuses a synonym list with more than 5,000 entries or with entries in the wrong language.
- Lukas wrote the rollback runbook and ran it once on production with the ramp at 0%, which proved only that the configuration rollout works. A real test needs traffic on Corvidex.

Problems: none blocking. The decision to ramp in steps means the migration finishes later than planned, which is the main reason for amber this week.

### Week 42 (12 to 16 October): 1% live

Overall: amber.

Corvidex went live for 1% of shoppers on Tuesday 2026-10-13 at 10:00. The step went through without errors, and the first shopper sessions on Corvidex were in the logs within a minute.

The first problem showed up the same afternoon. The 95th percentile latency for shoppers on Corvidex was 310 ms, against 162 ms in the mirrored test. Lukas traced it to the gateway instances in our second cloud region, which serve about a third of traffic and were calling Corvidex in the first region, because the Corvidex endpoint configuration listed only one region. The mirrored test had run only from the first region, so we had never seen it. With the second region pointed at Corvidex's endpoint in its own region, latency on Thursday was 171 ms. Corvidex charges for traffic between its regions, and the two days of cross-region calls cost about £1,100.

The gate report needed four days to get enough sessions on each device type at 1%, so the first full gate result came on Friday. All gates were green except conversion on mobile, which was 2.8% lower on Corvidex, relative. With so few sessions this was within the noise, and we decided to wait for 5% before reading anything into it.

Freshness was well inside the target all week. The 99th percentile delay was 1 minute 50 seconds. There were no price bursts larger than about a million changes this week.

Customer service saw two complaints that might have come from Corvidex results, both about an author search where a different author with a similar name came first. Both were for authors whose names differ only in an accent, which the Corvidex analyser was folding together. Saoirse's team added both pairs to a short list of names that must not be folded, and the list is in the relevance repository.

One thing went wrong in our own process. The 1% step was announced in #search-programme only on the morning of the step, not a working day ahead, because the announcement was in a draft that was never posted. Nothing broke, but the storefront teams were not watching. The announcement is now part of the step checklist in the gateway repository, and the step cannot be deployed without a link to it.

Decisions this week: none.

### Week 43 (19 to 23 October): 5% live, mobile conversion gap

Overall: amber.

We moved to 5% on Tuesday 2026-10-20 after three green days at 1% on every gate except mobile conversion, which the steering group agreed to wave through at 1% on the grounds that the sample was too small to mean anything. At 5% the samples are large enough after a day, and by Thursday the mobile conversion gap was clear and steady: 3.4% lower on Corvidex, relative, on both the iOS and Android apps and on the mobile web storefront. Desktop conversion was level, within 0.3%.

Ifeoma and Bram spent most of the week on the mobile gap. The search results themselves were not the problem. Shoppers who got to a results page converted at the same rate on both engines. The difference was in how many got there. On mobile, about 58% of searches start from autocomplete, against 31% on desktop, and Corvidex's autocomplete was ordering suggestions by text match only. Folio ordered them by text match and then by sales rank, so a shopper who typed "the sec" on Folio saw the best-selling title starting with those words first, and on Corvidex saw whichever title matched the letters best, which was often an old reference book. On a phone, with room for only four suggestions, that matters a lot.

Bram's team has a fix that sends sales rank into Corvidex's autocomplete scoring. It is built and is in testing on staging. It needs one change from Corvidex, a setting on our account that lets autocomplete use a numeric field, which their support team said would take up to 5 working days.

Zero-result rate on Corvidex at 5% was 5.1% against 6.8% on Folio, so that gate is comfortably green. Latency was 168 ms at the 95th percentile, also green. The Dutch storefront ran at 231 ms, inside its 300 ms exception.

Series queries remain weak on both engines. Saoirse's team built a parser that recognises series numbers in a query ("book 3", "vol. 3", "#3", "deel 3" in Dutch, and the word forms up to twelve) and turns them into a filter on the series number field. On the judged query set it raised the score for series queries from 0.48 to 0.66, but it also broke 9 queries for titles that contain a number, such as novels with a year in the title. The parser now only applies when the rest of the query matches a known series name, and it goes into the week 45 relevance release.

The indexer had its first real test of a price burst on Wednesday, when a large supplier sent a new price file with 1.4 million changes. The 99th percentile freshness delay went up to 4 minutes 20 seconds for about half an hour and came back down on its own. Dev noted in the weekly sync that this was closer to the 5 minute target than he liked, and that a larger burst would push it over. In hindsight this was the warning for week 44.

Other work this week:

- The judged query set was extended from 2,000 to 2,400 queries, adding 400 from the Dutch storefront, which had been under-represented.
- Folio's nightly rebuild failed on Thursday night because a disk filled up on one of the Folio servers. Folio served the previous day's index until the delta job caught up. The disk was the third Folio disk failure this year, and a reminder of why we are moving.
- Customer service now has a short note on how to tell whether a shopper's complaint came from Corvidex, using the session hash shown on the support console.

:::decision{status=accepted date=2026-10-22}
Decisions this week: the steering group agreed on 2026-10-22 to move to 25% on Tuesday 2026-10-27 if the autocomplete fix is live on Corvidex by then and the mobile gap has narrowed to under 2%. If not, the step waits a week.
:::

Correction (added 2026-10-30): this entry first said the mobile gap was 3.8%. The correct figure for the week is 3.4%, after removing a day of bot traffic that the analytics filter missed.

### Week 44 (26 to 30 October): 25% live, indexing incident

Overall: red on Thursday, back to amber by Friday.

Corvidex turned on the autocomplete setting on Monday, two days earlier than they promised. The autocomplete fix went live for shoppers on Corvidex on Monday afternoon, and by Tuesday morning the mobile conversion gap had narrowed to 1.6%. We moved to 25% on Tuesday 2026-10-27 at 10:00, as agreed by the steering group.

On Thursday 2026-10-29, from 13:10 UK time, the warehouse system and two suppliers posted price and stock files within the same hour, with 3.8 million changes in about 40 minutes. The indexer was sending every change as a full document rewrite, which at that volume used the whole Corvidex update quota for the UK index. The indexer's queue grew faster than it drained, and the 99th percentile freshness delay reached 47 minutes at 14:05. For that period, shoppers on Corvidex saw prices and stock that were up to 47 minutes old. Folio, which was 20 to 30 minutes behind as usual, did better than Corvidex for once.

The on-call, Lukas, rolled back from 25% to 5% at 14:12, which took 2 minutes 40 seconds. We did not roll back to 0% because at 5% the number of shoppers affected was small, and the A/B comparison for the step was still useful. The queue drained by 15:30, and freshness was back under 5 minutes by 15:45. We went back up to 25% at 20:00 on Thursday, which broke the rule that ramp steps only happen on Tuesday or Wednesday daytime. The programme lead approved it in the incident channel, on the grounds that returning to a percentage we had already been running is not a new step. The steering group has since agreed with that reading, but asked that it be written down, which it now is: returning to a percentage that has already passed its gates, within 24 hours of a rollback, counts as part of the rollback and not as a ramp step.

What it cost: we estimate that about 2,100 orders were placed by shoppers on Corvidex at a price that had changed in the previous 47 minutes. Our checkout always charges the current price from the price service, never the price shown in search, so no shopper was charged a wrong price. The effect was that 380 shoppers saw a higher price at checkout than in search, and customer service received 41 contacts about it. About 90 orders were placed for items that had just gone out of stock, and the warehouse cancelled them the next morning with the usual apology email.

The fix went into the indexer on Friday. Price and stock changes now go to Corvidex as partial updates, which are much cheaper, and the indexer gives them priority over metadata rewrites when its queue is longer than 50,000 changes. In a replay of Thursday's burst on staging, the 99th percentile freshness delay peaked at 3 minutes 5 seconds. The incident review is scheduled for 2026-11-04, and the "Important" note in "Quality gates" about 14 days of green freshness comes from the steering group's response to the incident.

Other work this week:

- The mobile conversion gap was 1.6% at the end of Tuesday and 1.3% on Friday. Desktop was level.
- Zero-result rate at 25% was 4.8% on Corvidex and 6.8% on Folio.
- The series number parser passed review and is scheduled for the relevance release on 2026-11-03.

:::decision{status=accepted date=2026-10-29}
Decisions this week: the rollback reading described above (accepted by the steering group on 2026-10-29).
:::

### Week 45 (2 to 6 November): holding at 25%

Overall: amber.

A quiet week on traffic, which is what we wanted. Corvidex has served 25% all week with no rollback. The partial update path for prices and stock has carried four bursts of more than a million changes, the largest 2.2 million on Wednesday morning, and the 99th percentile freshness delay stayed under 3 minutes 30 seconds through all of them. Over the week as a whole it was 3 minutes 10 seconds. The freshness gate has now been green for 7 days in a row, counting from Friday 2026-10-30, so the 14 days the steering group asked for end on Thursday 2026-11-12 at the earliest.

The relevance release went out on Tuesday 2026-11-03 with the series number parser, about 300 more synonym removals for the UK list, and the two accent pairs from week 42. The judged query set score went from 0.72 to 0.74, and the zero-result rate for series queries on live traffic dropped from 12.1% to 9.6% in three days. The overall zero-result rate at 25% is now 4.6% against Folio's 6.8%. On the UK storefront alone it is 4.1%, which is close to the 4% goal, while the Irish storefront is at 5.9% because of the larger share of restricted titles that shoppers search for and cannot find.

The mobile conversion gap is 1.1%, inside the 2% gate. Desktop conversion is 0.4% higher on Corvidex, which is within the noise. Revenue per search is level within 0.5%.

The incident review for 2026-10-29 took place on Wednesday 2026-11-04. Its main findings were that the indexer was load tested with steady traffic but never with bursts, that the price and stock service had no way to warn the indexer of a large file before sending it, and that the 5 minute freshness target was not on the on-call dashboard, so the on-call learned of the delay from a customer service message rather than an alert. Actions from the review are in "Actions" below. Lukas tested the rollback again on 2026-11-03 at 25%, and it took 2 minutes 10 seconds.

Spend: the Corvidex invoice for October came in at £61,400, against £52,000 in the budget for the month. The difference is the cross-region traffic in week 42, a higher query volume than we assumed because the mirrored test ran into October, and the update volume during the 2026-10-29 burst. The steering group accepted the overrun on 2026-10-29 when it saw the forecast, which puts the programme 6% over its budget for the year.

The peak question did not get decided. The steering group discussed it on Thursday 2026-11-05 and asked for one more week of numbers at 25%, including the freshness data from the 14-day window, before it decides. That leaves very little time before the freeze. The 14 green days end on Thursday 2026-11-12, and the next Tuesday is 2026-11-17, which is already inside the freeze. A 50% step before the peak can therefore only happen if the group agrees to cut the 14 days short and step on Wednesday 2026-11-11. Ottilie has asked the group for a decision by message by 2026-11-10.

Other work this week:

- Dev's team added a burst warning to the price and stock service. When it is about to send a file with more than 500,000 changes, it posts the size and expected start time to the indexer, and the indexer raises its partial update priority ahead of time.
- The freshness delay is now on the search on-call dashboard, with an alert at 4 minutes for 10 minutes in a row.
- Ifeoma produced the first weekly gate report broken down by storefront rather than overall, which is how we found the Irish zero-result rate problem.
- Folio's decommissioning plan is drafted (see "Folio decommissioning") and is with the security team for review.

Decisions this week: none final. The peak traffic question is still open.

