## Risks

:::risk{impact=high likelihood=medium}
Risk: a price or stock burst larger than anything seen so far pushes freshness past 5 minutes again, during the peak when prices change most. Impact high, likelihood medium. The partial update path has handled 2.2 million changes in one burst, and the replay of the 3.8 million burst stayed under target, but suppliers send their largest price files in the week before Black Friday, and last year's largest was 6.1 million changes. Dev's team is replaying a 7 million change burst on staging in week 46. If freshness goes past 5 minutes, the on-call rolls back to 5%.
:::

:::risk{impact=high likelihood=low}
Risk: Corvidex has an outage during the peak. Impact high, likelihood low. Their availability over the last 12 months, from their own status history, was 99.97%. With Folio still running and the rollback tested, a Corvidex outage costs us the time it takes to notice and roll back, which is under 10 minutes with the new alerting. The gateway's 800 ms timeout and category page fallback cover the minutes before the rollback.
:::

:::risk{impact=high likelihood=medium}
Risk: Folio fails during the peak, now that it carries less traffic and gets less attention. Impact high, likelihood medium. Folio had three disk failures this year and nobody on the team knows its query servers well. If Folio fails while it carries 75% of traffic, the gateway can be set to send 100% to Corvidex, but that is a jump from 25% that no gate has tested. Lukas is writing a separate runbook for this case, and the steering group has agreed in principle that a forced move to 100% is allowed when Folio is down, under the same rule as a rollback.
:::

:::risk{impact=medium likelihood=low}
Risk: the Folio lease end slips into the busy months, or the data removal is not finished by 2027-03-31. Impact medium, likelihood low. The lease cannot be extended month by month, and the data centre provider charges a full year if we overrun by a single day. The plan leaves six weeks between switching Folio off and the lease end.
:::

:::risk{impact=medium likelihood=high}
Risk: shoppers on the Irish storefront search for restricted titles more than we expected, and the zero-result gate never gets under 4% there. Impact medium, likelihood high. The restriction list is a legal requirement, so the fix cannot be to show the titles. Hollis is working with the content team on a "not available in Ireland" result card that would count as a result rather than a zero-result page, which needs the steering group's agreement on how the gate is measured.
:::

## Open questions

:::question
Undecided: how much traffic Corvidex carries through the peak trading freeze, from 2026-11-16 to 2026-12-04. Holding at 25% is the safe option for the peak itself, but it leaves Folio carrying three quarters of Black Friday traffic, and Folio is the system that failed last year. Going to 50% before the freeze means cutting the 14-day freshness proof short. The steering group will decide by message by 2026-11-10. Until then, the plan assumes Corvidex stays at 25% through the freeze, Folio's query servers are kept at full size, and the 50% step is scheduled for 2026-12-08.
:::

:::question
Undecided: whether the "not available in Ireland" result card counts as a result for the zero-result gate. Until then, the gate counts it as a zero-result search, and the Irish storefront's goal of 4% is reported but not used to block the 100% step.
:::

:::question
Not decided yet, but not blocking: whether we use the break clause in the Corvidex contract after the first year. The break clause has to be used by 2027-04-30. Nobody expects to, and nothing in this plan depends on it.
:::

## Folio decommissioning

Folio is switched off in three steps after the 100% step, and the hardware goes back to the data centre provider before the lease ends on 2027-03-31.

1. For 30 days after the 100% step, Folio keeps running without traffic, and its delta job keeps it up to date, so that a rollback still returns shoppers to a fresh index. The rollback rule stays as it is during these 30 days.
2. After the 30 days, the Folio delta job and nightly rebuild are stopped, and the gateway configuration for Folio is removed. From this point there is no rollback to Folio.
3. The Folio servers are wiped by the data centre provider's certified process, and the provider gives us a certificate for each disk. Lukas collects the certificates and sends them to the security team.

Folio's index holds no customer data, but its query logs do. They contain query text, session IDs and, for searches made before 2023, the account ID of logged-in shoppers. The query logs are not copied anywhere before the wipe, except for an anonymised sample of 2 million queries from 2025 and 2026, which Saoirse's team keeps for the judged query set and which the privacy team has approved. Account IDs are removed from the sample before it leaves the Folio servers.

The 18 Folio servers are leased, so they are not ours to keep or sell. The 4 network switches in the Folio racks are ours and move to the main data centre room, where the network team will reuse them.

Folio's code stays in the archive repository, read only. Nobody should start a new project on it. Some of its ISBN handling code is still used by the catalogue service's import job, and Dev's team will move that code into the catalogue service before the archive is frozen.

## Operations and on-call

The search on-call rota has six people from the search programme and the gateway team, one week each. During the ramp the on-call covers both engines, the gateway and the indexer. Folio's own servers are still covered by the infrastructure on-call, as they have been since 2019, and the search on-call pages them for anything below the Folio application.

The on-call dashboard shows, for each engine and storefront, the query rate, the 95th percentile latency, the error and timeout rate, the zero-result rate for the last hour, and the freshness delay for Corvidex. It also shows the current ramp percentage and the time of the last gateway configuration change, which helped twice in week 44 when people asked whether a change was a ramp step or a rollback.

Alerts that page the search on-call:

- Corvidex or Folio error and timeout rate above 1% for 5 minutes on any storefront.
- Gateway 95th percentile latency above 600 ms for 10 minutes.
- Corvidex freshness delay above 4 minutes for 10 minutes in a row (new in week 45).
- The indexer's queue above 500,000 changes.
- Folio's nightly rebuild not finished by 07:00 UK time.

The on-call does not change ranking, synonyms or pinned results in an incident. If a ranking problem is serious enough to need action out of hours, for example an offensive title coming first for a common query, the on-call rolls the affected storefront back to Folio and leaves the fix to Saoirse's team in working hours. Rolling back a single storefront is possible because each storefront has its own ramp percentage in the gateway configuration, although today all three are set the same.

Corvidex support is reachable through their support portal for anything that is not urgent, and by phone for an outage, with a response time of 30 minutes on our contract tier. Only the people on the search on-call rota and the programme lead are registered contacts, so other teams who need Corvidex support go through the on-call.

### Access and keys

Corvidex has two kinds of API key on our account. Query keys can only search, and the gateway holds one per storefront. Admin keys can change indexes, settings and ranking, and only the indexer and the relevance deploy pipeline hold them. Both kinds are stored in the secrets manager and rotated every 90 days by the platform team's rotation job. No person has an admin key. Changes to the Corvidex account that need a person, such as raising a quota, go through the console with single sign-on, and the console roles for our team are read-only for ranking, as described in "How results are ordered".

The query keys are restricted to the gateway's outbound addresses, so a leaked query key cannot be used from anywhere else. Admin keys are restricted the same way to the indexer's and the pipeline's addresses. A key is never put in a storefront page, a mobile app or a browser request, including for autocomplete, which goes through the gateway like everything else.

Folio has no keys. Anything inside the Eastfield network could query it, which was acceptable in 2016 and is one of the findings the security team raised in its 2025 review. Since Folio is going away, the finding was accepted as a risk until the switch-off rather than fixed.

## Search reporting after the migration

Merchandising and the buying team use search reports every week: the most common searches, the most common zero-result searches, and searches that lead to a product page but not a purchase. Folio produced these from its own query logs, in a nightly job that wrote spreadsheets to a shared drive. That job stops working for the share of traffic on Corvidex, and it stops completely when Folio is switched off.

The replacement reports come from the analytics warehouse, from the same search events as the gate report, so they cover both engines during the ramp. Ifeoma's team built them in September, and merchandising has been using them alongside the old spreadsheets since week 42. The two disagree slightly, because the warehouse leaves out bots and staff sessions and Folio's job did not. Merchandising has agreed to use the warehouse numbers from 2026-11-02 onwards.

The zero-result report is the one that matters most to the buying team, because a common search with no results is often a book we should stock. The warehouse report lists each zero-result query with the storefront and the count, and since week 45 it also says whether the query matched a restricted title, so the buying team stops asking about books we are not allowed to sell in Ireland.

Search reports do not include anything that identifies a shopper. A query that looks like an email address, a phone number or a postcode is replaced with a placeholder before it reaches the warehouse.

## Questions we get asked

Will shoppers see different prices on Corvidex? No. The price in the results comes from the index, which is updated from the price service within the freshness target, and checkout always charges the current price from the price service. During the 2026-10-29 incident, shoppers saw old prices in results, but nobody was charged one.

Can my team get a search API for an internal tool? Not during the migration. After the 100% step we expect to offer read-only access through the gateway for internal tools, but not direct access to Corvidex.

Why is the Dutch storefront slower? Because every query is analysed in Dutch and English. It is inside its own 300 ms target, and the latency gate has an exception for it.

Can we pin a title for a promotion tomorrow? Yes, through the pinned results form, within the limit of 3 per query and 200 queries per storefront, and with an end date. A pin raised before 14:00 on a working day is normally live the same day.

Why does a book show as "out of print" in search but someone is selling it on the marketplace? Because marketplace offers are shown on the result card of the edition they belong to, and the edition itself is out of print. The card shows both. If it does not, that is a bug, and #search-programme wants to hear about it.

What happens to search if Corvidex goes down during the peak? The on-call rolls back to Folio. Until then, the gateway shows the category page for a query when an engine does not answer within 800 ms.

