The mobile apps needed no release for the migration, because they have called the gateway since 2023. That will not stay true if we want the format hint on the apps, which is sent from the browser on the web storefronts but not from the apps. That is a small change for a later app release and not part of this plan.

The catalogue team has frozen the change feed format until the end of January, apart from fixes. Any new field they add before then goes to Folio and Corvidex through the same feed, so the indexer has to ignore fields it does not know rather than fail on them. It does, and there is a test for it.

## Budget

The budget agreed in May covers the Corvidex contract for the first year, the engineering time of the programme team, and the cost of running Folio until it is switched off.

| Item | Budget for 2026-27 | Forecast | Difference |
|-|-|-|-|
| Corvidex service (queries, indexing, storage) | £540,000 | £592,000 | +£52,000 |
| Corvidex cross-region traffic | £0 | £1,100 | +£1,100 |
| Folio hardware lease and power, to 2027-03-31 | £168,000 | £168,000 | £0 |
| Programme team (7 people, internal cost) | £610,000 | £640,000 | +£30,000 |
| Judging for the query set | £24,000 | £21,500 | −£2,500 |
| Contingency | £60,000 | £0 | −£60,000 |
| Total | £1,402,000 | £1,422,600 | +£20,600 |

The forecast assumes 100% on Corvidex from 2027-01-12. Each extra month at a split between the engines costs about £14,000 more than the forecast, mostly in Corvidex query volume that we pay for on top of Folio's fixed costs.

The table nets the overrun against the unused contingency. Against the budget without contingency, which is how the finance team reports it, we are at 6% over. The programme team line is higher because the stepped ramp needs Ifeoma's analysis work for about ten weeks longer than the single switch would have.

Corvidex invoices monthly in arrears. Query volume is billed per thousand queries, with the price per thousand dropping above 120 million queries a month. We are at about 35 million a month on Corvidex at 25%, so we will only reach the cheaper tier at about 100%.

## How the gate report is made

The gate report runs every morning at 06:00 UK time over the previous three full days, and Ifeoma's team checks it before 09:30. It reads search events from the analytics warehouse, where every search the gateway serves is logged with the session hash, the engine, the storefront, the device type and whether the session later ended in a purchase. A session counts as a search session if it has at least one search, and it is attributed to the engine that served its first search. Because a shopper's bucket never changes within a session, mixed sessions only happen when the ramp changes, and the report leaves out sessions that crossed a configuration change.

The core of the conversion gate looks like this. The real query also filters out bots, staff sessions and test storefronts.

```sql
SELECT
  engine,
  device_type,
  COUNT(*)                                   AS search_sessions,
  AVG(CASE WHEN purchased THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS conversion
FROM analytics.search_sessions
WHERE session_date BETWEEN CURRENT_DATE - 3 AND CURRENT_DATE - 1
  AND crossed_config_change = FALSE
  AND is_bot = FALSE
GROUP BY engine, device_type
HAVING COUNT(*) >= 40000;
```

The report shows each gate as green, red or "not enough data". "Not enough data" never counts as green. Ifeoma's team added a check in week 43 that a single day cannot make up more than half of a gate's sessions, after a day of bot traffic that the filter missed made the mobile gap look worse than it was.

Shoppers who block analytics cookies are not in the report at all, since their sessions are not logged with a purchase outcome. They are about 11% of sessions, spread evenly across the two engines by the hash, so we do not expect them to change the comparison.

