## Communication

Every ramp step and every rollback is announced in #search-programme, and the weekly report goes to the steering group and the heads of the storefront, catalogue and customer service teams on Friday afternoon. Customer service gets a separate short note before each ramp step, written for their agents, with what shoppers might notice and the saved replies to use.

We do not tell shoppers that search is changing. There is no banner, no blog post and no mention in the newsletter. Shoppers who notice different results will mostly see better ones, and announcing a new search engine invites comparison with the old one in the worst week to have it. The exception is the trade and library customers on the business storefront pages, who search by ISBN lists in bulk. They get a short email from their account managers before the 100% step, because the bulk ISBN search now ignores invalid ISBNs instead of failing the whole list.

Questions from outside the programme go to Ottilie, or to #search-programme. Please do not contact Corvidex directly about our account. They are polite about it, but their account manager then calls the programme lead to ask what is going on.

## What we have learned so far

These are the points from the first five weeks of the ramp that we would want the next migration of this size to know. Some of them are already in the incident review, but most are not incidents.

The mirrored test was worth doing for latency and load, and nearly useless for relevance. Comparing two engines' results without shoppers in the loop told us that the results were different, which we knew, and not which were better. If we did it again we would mirror for one week, not two, and start the live ramp at 1% a week earlier.

Measure by device type from day one. The overall conversion number at 1% and 5% looked fine, because desktop was level and slightly better, and it hid a mobile gap that we only saw because Ifeoma had split the report by device type out of habit.

Autocomplete is half of search on mobile. We planned the migration as if the results page was the product and autocomplete was a detail, and the biggest regression we found was in autocomplete.

Load test the indexer with bursts, not steady traffic. The steady load test passed at three times our normal update rate, and the first large burst still broke freshness, because what matters is the queue during a burst, not the average rate.

Write down the rules for rollback before you need them. The rule that anyone on call can roll back without asking worked exactly as intended in week 44. The rule we had not written down, about returning to a percentage after a rollback, caused a half-hour argument in the incident channel while the queue was draining.

Keep Folio boring. Every week someone suggests a small improvement to Folio, since it still serves most traffic. We have said no each time, apart from replacing failed disks. The one change we made to Folio this year, a memory setting in August, is the change we suspect for the disk filling up in week 43, though we never proved it.

## Notes from the steering group meeting on 2026-10-29 (meeting notes, for people) {agent=skip}

Present: Imogen Hartley (chief digital officer, chair), Ottilie Brand, Hollis Ng, Lukas Ferreira, Ifeoma Chukwu, Anouk Verbeek for the catalogue team, Grainne Doyle for customer service, and Pieter Jansen from finance. Dev joined for the first item from the incident channel, since the queue was still draining when the meeting started at 15:00.

The meeting was mostly about the incident earlier that afternoon. Lukas described the rollback to 5% and the state of the queue. Imogen asked whether we should go back to 0% until the fix was in. Lukas and Ottilie argued against it. At 5% the shoppers affected were few, Folio was no fresher than Corvidex at its usual 20 to 30 minutes behind, and dropping to 0% would throw away the 5% comparison data. Grainne said customer service could handle the contact volume at 5%. Imogen agreed, on the condition that we did not return to 25% until the queue was empty and freshness was back under target for at least an hour, which is what happened at 20:00.

There was a long discussion about whether returning to 25% the same evening was a ramp step. Hollis read out the rule that steps happen on Tuesday or Wednesday daytime. Ottilie's view was that the rule was written to stop us moving to a new, untested percentage when the team is not around, and that 25% had already passed its gates. Pieter asked, reasonably, whether the rule would end up meaning whatever was convenient at the time. The compromise was to write down the 24-hour reading in the Weekly status so that it is a rule and not a judgement call.

Anouk raised the price and stock bursts from the catalogue side. The burst was not unusual for a Thursday at the end of the month, and suppliers will send bigger ones in November. She offered to have the price and stock service warn the indexer ahead of large files, which became the burst warning that went live in week 45.

Pieter presented the cost forecast. The October invoice would be about £9,000 over the month's budget, and the year about 6% over without contingency. Imogen accepted the overrun, saying the cost of the 2025 outage was many times larger and that she would rather pay for a careful ramp than save money on a fast one. Pieter asked for the monthly forecast to be in the weekly report from now on, which it is, in "Budget".

The last item, the peak question, was moved to the following week because the meeting ran over. It was discussed on 2026-11-05 and is still open.

## Glossary

- Bucket: one of the 1,000 groups that the gateway's router puts sessions into by session hash. The ramp percentage decides how many buckets go to Corvidex.
- Edition: one ISBN, or one product code for items without an ISBN.
- Work: a book as a whole, grouping all its editions and formats.
- Freshness: the delay between the catalogue publishing a change and the change showing in search.
- Gate: one of the quality measures in "Quality gates" that must be green before a ramp step.
- Judged query set: the 2,400 queries with graded results that we use to score ranking offline.
- Partial update: an update that changes only some fields of an index document, used for price and stock since week 45.
- Peak trading freeze: the period from 2026-11-16 to 2026-12-04 when no changes are made to the storefronts or their services except fixes for incidents.
- Ramp step: a planned increase in the share of traffic that Corvidex serves.
- Rollback: setting the ramp percentage back, usually to 0, so that Folio serves the traffic.
- Zero-result rate: the share of searches that return no results at all.
